exec(open(__file__.rsplit("/",1)[0] + "/_h.py").read())
B("Hosea", """
1|God tells Hosea to marry Gomer, an unfaithful woman, as a picture of Israel's unfaithfulness. Their children are given names that mean God's judgment, but he promises that Israel will be restored.
2|Israel is like an unfaithful wife who runs after other lovers. God will lead her into the wilderness and speak tenderly to her. I will betroth you to me forever.
3|Hosea buys back his wife and tells her to wait for him. This shows God's love for Israel, who will wait many days without a king before returning.
4|God has a case against Israel: no faithfulness, no love, no knowledge of God. Priests and people sin together. My people are destroyed for lack of knowledge.
5|God condemns the priests and the royal house. Ephraim is oppressed and Judah is also guilty. God withdraws until they seek him.
6|Come, let us return to the Lord. I desire steadfast love and not sacrifice, the knowledge of God rather than burnt offerings.
7|Ephraim is like a flat cake, half-baked, and like a silly dove, calling to Egypt and Assyria for help. They do not cry to God from the heart.
8|They sow the wind and reap the whirlwind. Israel has made kings and idols without God.
9|Israel will go into exile. The prophet is called a fool, but the days of punishment have come.
10|Israel is a luxuriant vine that multiplied altars. Sow righteousness and reap steadfast love. It is time to seek the Lord.
11|When Israel was a child I loved him, and out of Egypt I called my son. God taught him to walk. How can I give you up? God will not destroy them in anger.
12|Jacob struggled with his brother in the womb and with God. God calls Israel to return and hold fast to love and justice.
13|Ephraim was great but sinned with Baal and died. God says: I will ransom them from the power of Sheol. O death, where are your plagues?
14|Return, O Israel, to the Lord your God. God promises to heal their faithlessness and love them freely. Whoever is wise understands these things.
""")
B("Joel", """
1|A plague of locusts devastates the land. The priests are told to put on sackcloth and call a solemn assembly. The day of the Lord is near.
2|The day of the Lord is coming, with an army like fire. Return to me with all your heart. God will repay the years the locusts have eaten, and pour out his Spirit on all flesh. Sons and daughters will prophesy.
3|God will gather the nations in the Valley of Jehoshaphat for judgment. He will dwell in Zion, and the mountains will drip with sweet wine.
""")
B("Amos", """
1|Amos, a shepherd from Tekoa, announces God's judgment on Damascus, Gaza, Tyre, Edom and Ammon for their cruelty.
2|Judgment on Moab, Judah and Israel. Israel sells the righteous for silver and the needy for sandals, and tramples the heads of the poor.
3|God has chosen Israel, so he will hold her accountable. Do two walk together unless they have agreed? The lion roars, who will not fear?
4|The women of Samaria, the cows of Bashan, oppress the poor. God sent famine and drought, but they did not return to him. Prepare to meet your God.
5|Seek me and live. Let justice roll down like waters and righteousness like an ever-flowing stream. God hates their feasts and songs when there is no justice.
6|Woe to those at ease in Zion. They lie on beds of ivory and are not grieved over the ruin of Joseph.
7|Visions of locusts and fire, which Amos prays against, and a plumb line. Amaziah the priest tells Amos to leave. Amos says he was a herdsman and God called him.
8|A basket of summer fruit shows that Israel is ripe for the end. God will send a famine of hearing the words of the Lord.
9|God will strike the temple and no one will escape. But in that day I will raise up the fallen tent of David, and Israel will be planted in her land.
""")
B("Obadiah", """
1|A message of judgment on Edom, who was proud and gloated over Jerusalem's fall, and attacked his brother Jacob. The day of the Lord is near, and the kingdom shall be the Lord's.
""")
B("Jonah", """
1|God tells Jonah to go to Nineveh. Jonah flees by ship toward Tarshish. A great storm comes, the sailors throw him overboard, and a great fish swallows him.
2|Inside the fish, Jonah prays. Salvation belongs to the Lord. God commands the fish to spit Jonah onto dry land.
3|Jonah goes to Nineveh and says it will be overthrown in forty days. The people repent from the king down, and God changes his mind about the disaster.
4|Jonah is angry because he knew God is gracious and merciful. God makes a plant grow and then die to teach him: should I not pity Nineveh, that great city?
""")
B("Micah", """
1|Micah announces the fall of Samaria and warns that the disaster will reach Jerusalem. He mourns and calls on the towns to weep.
2|Woe to those who plan evil on their beds and seize fields and houses. God promises to gather the remnant of Israel like sheep.
3|Micah condemns the rulers who hate good and love evil, and the false prophets who say Peace when they are fed. Zion will be plowed like a field.
4|In the last days the mountain of the Lord will be raised up. They will beat their swords into plowshares. Everyone will sit under his own vine and fig tree.
5|From Bethlehem Ephrathah will come a ruler of Israel, whose origin is from of old. He will stand and shepherd his flock in the strength of the Lord.
6|The Lord has a case against his people. He has told you what is good: to do justice, to love kindness and to walk humbly with your God.
7|Micah mourns that the godly have vanished. Yet I will look to the Lord. Who is a God like you, pardoning iniquity? He will cast all our sins into the depths of the sea.
""")
B("Nahum", """
1|The Lord is slow to anger and a stronghold in the day of trouble. He is jealous and avenging, and will end Nineveh. Good news: peace for Judah.
2|The attack on Nineveh is described. The shields are red and the chariots flash like torches. The lion's den is empty.
3|Woe to the bloody city, full of lies and plunder. Nineveh will fall like Thebes, and no one will mourn her.
""")
B("Habakkuk", """
1|Habakkuk asks God how long he will tolerate violence. God says he is raising up the Chaldeans (Babylon). Habakkuk asks how God can use a people more wicked than Judah.
2|God answers: write the vision. The righteous shall live by his faith. Five woes against the proud and greedy. The Lord is in his holy temple, so be silent before him.
3|Habakkuk's prayer. God comes in power from Teman. Though the fig tree does not blossom, yet I will rejoice in the Lord. The Lord God is my strength.
""")
B("Zephaniah", """
1|The day of the Lord is near. God will sweep away everything from the earth and punish Judah for idolatry, and those who say the Lord will do nothing, good or bad.
2|Seek the Lord, all you humble of the land. Judgment on the Philistines, Moab, Ammon, Ethiopia and Nineveh.
3|Woe to Jerusalem, the oppressing city. God will purify the peoples' lips. The Lord your God is in your midst. He will rejoice over you with gladness and sing over you with loud singing.
""")
B("Haggai", """
1|The people have rebuilt their own houses but not the temple. Haggai tells them to consider their ways. Zerubbabel and Joshua the high priest begin the work.
2|The new temple seems small, but God says: I will fill this house with glory. The glory of this house will be greater than the former. God promises to bless them from that day on.
""")
B("Zechariah", """
1|Zechariah calls the people to return to God. He sees a man on a red horse among myrtle trees, and God promises to be merciful to Jerusalem.
2|A man with a measuring line is going to measure Jerusalem. God will be a wall of fire around it and will dwell in its midst. Many nations will join themselves to the Lord.
3|The high priest Joshua stands in dirty clothes before the angel of the Lord, and Satan accuses him. God removes his guilt and clothes him in clean garments. The Branch is coming.
4|A golden lampstand with two olive trees. Not by might, nor by power, but by my Spirit, says the Lord. Zerubbabel will finish the temple.
5|A flying scroll shows the curse on thieves and liars. A woman in a basket, a symbol of wickedness, is carried away to Babylon.
6|Four chariots go out to the earth. Joshua is crowned, and a man named the Branch will build the temple and rule on his throne as priest and king.
7|The people ask whether they should still fast. God says: show justice and mercy, not fasting for show. Their ancestors refused to listen.
8|God will return to Zion and dwell in Jerusalem. Old men and women will sit in its streets, and children will play. Ten men will take hold of one Jew and say: we will go with you.
9|The coming king: rejoice greatly, O daughter of Zion, behold your king is coming to you, humble and mounted on a donkey. He will speak peace to the nations.
10|Ask the Lord for rain. The false shepherds are punished, and God will bring his people back from Egypt and Assyria.
11|The shepherd of the flock is rejected. He is paid thirty pieces of silver, which he throws into the house of the Lord to the potter.
12|God will make Jerusalem a cup of staggering to the nations. They will look on the one they have pierced and mourn for him as for an only child.
13|A fountain will be opened to cleanse sin. Strike the shepherd and the sheep will be scattered. A third of the people will be refined like silver.
14|The day of the Lord: God stands on the Mount of Olives, which splits in two. Living waters flow out of Jerusalem. The Lord will be king over all the earth.
""")
B("Malachi", """
1|God says: I have loved you. Yet the priests offer defective animals and despise his name. My name will be great among the nations, and a pure offering will be offered in every place.
2|God warns the priests to give honor to his name. The people are unfaithful to their wives and have profaned the covenant. The Lord hates divorce.
3|I send my messenger to prepare the way before me. The Lord will suddenly come to his temple. Bring the full tithe into the storehouse, and see if I will not open the windows of heaven.
4|The day is coming, burning like an oven. For you who fear my name, the sun of righteousness will rise with healing in its wings. God will send Elijah before the great day.
""")
B("1 Maccabees", """
1|Alexander the Great dies and his empire is divided. Antiochus Epiphanes comes to power, attacks Jerusalem, and forces the Jews to give up their laws. Many obey, but many resist.
2|Mattathias, a priest in Modein, refuses to offer pagan sacrifice and kills a Jew who does. He and his sons flee to the hills. On his deathbed he urges them to be zealous for the law and names Judas as leader.
3|Judas Maccabeus leads the revolt and wins early victories against Apollonius and Seron. Antiochus assembles a large army against him.
4|Judas defeats Gorgias and Lysias. The Jews retake Jerusalem, cleanse the temple, build a new altar and dedicate it with joy for eight days.
5|Judas fights the neighboring peoples who threaten the Jews. Simon rescues the Jews in Galilee. Judas and Jonathan rescue the Jews in Gilead.
6|Antiochus dies in Persia, regretting his evil. His son Antiochus V besieges the temple. Eleazar dies under an elephant. Lysias offers peace.
7|Demetrius becomes king. Alcimus, a priest, and Bacchides attack. Judas defeats Nicanor, who threatens the temple, and the day is celebrated each year.
8|Judas makes a treaty with Rome. The writer describes Roman power and its fairness to its allies.
9|Judas is killed in battle against Bacchides. Jonathan is chosen as leader, and fights on.
10|Jonathan is made high priest by Alexander Balas. Demetrius offers Jonathan great privileges, but Jonathan chooses Alexander. Alexander defeats Demetrius.
11|Ptolemy turns against Alexander. Demetrius II becomes king, and Jonathan wins his favor. Jonathan helps him and later Antiochus VI.
12|Jonathan renews the treaty with Rome and Sparta. Trypho tricks Jonathan and takes him prisoner at Ptolemais.
13|Simon becomes leader. Trypho kills Jonathan. Simon takes the Akra and Israel is free from Gentile rule. Simon builds a monument for his family.
14|Simon governs in peace. The people praise him and name him high priest, governor and leader. The people honor him by an inscription on bronze tablets.
15|Antiochus VII promises Simon favors, then turns against him. Rome supports the Jews in a letter. Simon rejects Antiochus' demands.
16|John Hyrcanus, Simon's son, defeats Cendebeus. Ptolemy murders Simon and two of his sons at a banquet. John escapes. The book ends with John becoming high priest.
""")
B("2 Maccabees", """
1|Two letters from the Jews in Jerusalem to the Jews in Egypt invite them to celebrate Hanukkah. The first describes how Nehemiah found the sacred fire hidden in a well.
2|More about the fire hidden by Jeremiah. The author says he is summarizing the five books of Jason of Cyrene.
3|Heliodorus, sent by King Seleucus, tries to take the temple treasury. Angels appear and beat him. The high priest Onias prays and Heliodorus is spared.
4|Jason buys the high priesthood and brings Greek customs to Jerusalem. Menelaus outbids Jason. Onias is murdered.
5|Antiochus attacks Jerusalem and plunders the temple. The writer says God allowed it because of the people's sins.
6|The temple is made a place for pagan worship. Eleazar, an old scribe, refuses to eat pork and dies willingly rather than disobey God.
7|A mother and her seven sons are tortured and killed because they will not eat pork. They say that the King of the world will raise them to everlasting life.
8|Judas Maccabeus and his men defeat Nicanor and Gorgias, relying on God's help.
9|Antiochus Epiphanes is struck by a painful illness and dies in agony in the mountains. He promises to become a Jew, but his repentance is too late.
10|Judas cleanses the temple and the Feast of Dedication is established. He defeats more enemies.
11|Lysias, the king's regent, is defeated by Judas, and then makes peace. Letters from the king and Rome are quoted.
12|Judas defeats several peoples. He collects money for a sin offering for the dead soldiers who had idols on them, in the hope that they will rise. It is a holy and pious thought to pray for the dead.
13|Antiochus V and Lysias attack Judea. Judas prays and wins at Modein. The king makes peace with the Jews.
14|Demetrius takes the throne. Alcimus tries to turn him against the Jews. Nicanor is sent. Razis kills himself rather than fall into the hands of his enemies.
15|Nicanor threatens the temple and is defeated. Judas has a dream in which Onias and Jeremiah appear to him. The writer ends with a hope that his work is well done.
""")
